"""Object-Oriented Programming questions (Java-style)."""
E, M, H = "Easy", "Medium", "Hard"

QUESTIONS = {
    "Classes and Objects": [
        (E, "Which keyword is used to create an object in Java?", "new", ["create", "alloc", "object"],
         "The new operator allocates memory and calls the constructor, returning a reference to the object."),
        (E, "A class is best described as:", "A blueprint from which objects are created", ["A running instance of a program", "A variable that stores an object's address", "A method that returns objects"],
         "A class defines the fields and methods; objects are the concrete instances created from it."),
        (M, "What is the output?\nclass Counter { static int count = 0; Counter() { count++; } }\nCounter a = new Counter();\nCounter b = new Counter();\nCounter c = new Counter();\nSystem.out.println(Counter.count);", "3", ["0", "1", "Compile-time error"],
         "count is static, so all objects share one copy; the constructor ran three times."),
        (M, "Which statement about static fields is true?", "A static field is shared by all objects of the class", ["Each object has its own copy of a static field", "Static fields can only be read inside instance methods", "Static fields are created only when the first object is created"],
         "Static fields belong to the class itself and exist once, even if no object has been created."),
        (H, "What is the output?\nclass P { int x = 10; }\nP p1 = new P();\nP p2 = p1;\np2.x = 20;\nSystem.out.println(p1.x);", "20", ["10", "30", "Compile-time error"],
         "p2 = p1 copies the reference, not the object; both variables point to the same object."),
    ],
    "Constructors": [
        (E, "What is the return type of a constructor?", "It has none, not even void", ["void", "int", "Object"],
         "A constructor has the class name and no return type; declaring one turns it into an ordinary method."),
        (E, "If a class defines no constructor, what does the Java compiler do?", "Supplies a default no-argument constructor", ["Reports a compile-time error", "Makes the class abstract", "Creates objects without running any initialisation"],
         "The compiler adds a public no-argument constructor only when the class declares none."),
        (M, "Defining several constructors with different parameter lists in one class is called:", "Constructor overloading", ["Constructor overriding", "Constructor chaining", "Constructor hiding"],
         "Overloading means the same name with different signatures; the compiler picks one from the arguments."),
        (M, "Which call invokes another constructor of the same class and must be the first statement of a constructor?", "this(...)", ["init(...)", "self(...)", "base(...)"],
         "this(...) chains to another constructor of the same class; super(...) chains to the parent's constructor."),
        (H, "What is the output?\nclass A { A() { System.out.print(\"A \"); } }\nclass B extends A { B() { System.out.print(\"B \"); } }\nnew B();", "A B", ["B A", "B", "A"],
         "The subclass constructor implicitly calls super() first, so the parent constructor prints before the child's."),
    ],
    "Encapsulation": [
        (E, "Which access modifier makes a member visible only inside its own class?", "private", ["protected", "public", "default (package-private)"],
         "private members can only be accessed by code in the same class."),
        (E, "Making fields private and exposing public getters and setters demonstrates which principle?", "Encapsulation", ["Polymorphism", "Inheritance", "Abstraction"],
         "Encapsulation hides internal state and allows controlled access through methods."),
        (M, "Why does a setter method usually validate its input?", "To ensure the object never enters an invalid state", ["To make the method run faster", "To allow the field to be accessed without an object", "To convert the class into an interface"],
         "Controlled mutation is a key benefit of encapsulation: bad values are rejected at the boundary."),
        (M, "A member declared without any access modifier in Java is accessible from:", "Classes in the same package", ["Only the same class", "Any class in any package", "Only subclasses in other packages"],
         "Default (package-private) access allows the same package but not other packages, even for subclasses."),
        (H, "Which is the strongest maintenance benefit of encapsulation?", "The internal representation can change without affecting client code", ["Objects use less memory", "Subclassing becomes unnecessary", "Methods no longer need parameters"],
         "Clients depend on the public interface only, so private implementation details can be changed freely."),
    ],
    "Inheritance": [
        (E, "Which keyword is used by a Java class to inherit from another class?", "extends", ["implements", "inherits", "super"],
         "class Dog extends Animal declares Animal as the superclass; implements is for interfaces."),
        (E, "Java does not allow a class to extend several classes. Which construct lets a class have several supertypes?", "Interfaces", ["Constructors", "Static blocks", "Packages"],
         "A class can implement any number of interfaces, which gives multiple inheritance of type."),
        (M, "What is method overriding?", "A subclass provides its own implementation of a method with the same signature as in its superclass", ["Defining methods with the same name but different parameters in one class", "Calling a method from inside a constructor", "Hiding a private field of the parent"],
         "Overriding redefines inherited behaviour and is resolved at runtime."),
        (M, "What is the output?\nclass Animal { void sound() { System.out.print(\"Generic \"); } }\nclass Dog extends Animal { void sound() { System.out.print(\"Bark \"); } }\nAnimal a = new Dog();\na.sound();", "Bark", ["Generic", "Generic Bark", "Compile-time error"],
         "The overridden method is chosen from the actual object (Dog), not the reference type (Animal)."),
        (H, "Which members of a superclass are NOT inherited by a subclass?", "Private members and constructors", ["Protected methods", "Public methods", "Public fields"],
         "Constructors are never inherited, and private members are not accessible in the subclass."),
    ],
    "Polymorphism": [
        (E, "Method overloading is an example of:", "Compile-time polymorphism", ["Runtime polymorphism", "Inheritance", "Encapsulation"],
         "The compiler selects the overloaded method from the argument types, so the binding is static."),
        (E, "Which of the following is an example of runtime polymorphism?", "Method overriding", ["Method overloading", "Variable shadowing", "Declaring final variables"],
         "Which overridden method runs is decided at runtime by the object's actual class (dynamic dispatch)."),
        (M, "What is the output?\nclass Calc {\n  int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }\n  double add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }\n}\nSystem.out.println(new Calc().add(2, 3.0));", "5.0", ["5", "Compile-time error", "Runtime error"],
         "The int argument is widened to double, so add(double, double) is chosen and returns 5.0."),
        (M, "When a superclass reference points to a subclass object, which factor decides which overridden method executes?", "The actual type of the object at runtime", ["The declared type of the reference", "The order of methods in the file", "The access modifier of the reference variable"],
         "Dynamic method dispatch uses the runtime class of the object."),
        (H, "What is the output?\nclass A { static void show() { System.out.print(\"A\"); } }\nclass B extends A { static void show() { System.out.print(\"B\"); } }\nA obj = new B();\nobj.show();", "A", ["B", "AB", "Compile-time error"],
         "Static methods are hidden, not overridden, so the call is bound at compile time to the reference type A."),
    ],
    "Abstraction": [
        (E, "Which of the following cannot be instantiated directly?", "An abstract class", ["A concrete class", "A final class", "A class with a constructor"],
         "Abstract classes are incomplete and must be extended by a concrete class."),
        (E, "A method declared abstract must be:", "Implemented by a concrete subclass", ["Declared private", "Declared static", "Declared final"],
         "An abstract method has no body; abstract cannot be combined with private, static or final."),
        (M, "Which statement about abstract classes is true?", "They can contain both abstract and concrete methods", ["All of their methods must be abstract", "They cannot have constructors", "They can be declared final"],
         "Abstract classes may include implemented methods, fields and constructors; final would prevent subclassing."),
        (M, "Which statement best distinguishes abstraction from encapsulation?", "Abstraction hides complexity by exposing essential behaviour; encapsulation hides data by restricting access", ["Abstraction restricts access to fields; encapsulation hides method implementations", "They are two names for the same concept", "Abstraction applies only to interfaces; encapsulation only to packages"],
         "Abstraction is a design-level idea about what to show; encapsulation is an implementation technique about protecting state."),
        (H, "Can an abstract class have a constructor?", "Yes; it runs when an object of a concrete subclass is created", ["No, because it cannot be instantiated", "Yes, but it can never be called", "Only if it is declared static"],
         "A subclass constructor calls the abstract parent's constructor via super(), which initialises inherited state."),
    ],
    "Interfaces": [
        (E, "Which keyword does a class use to implement an interface?", "implements", ["extends", "inherits", "uses"],
         "class A implements Runnable promises to provide all of the interface's abstract methods."),
        (E, "By default, fields declared in a Java interface are:", "public static final", ["private and non-static", "protected static", "public and non-final"],
         "Interface fields are implicitly constants."),
        (M, "Since Java 8, which kinds of methods in an interface may have a body?", "default and static methods", ["Only abstract methods", "Only constructors", "Only private final methods"],
         "default methods provide inherited behaviour without breaking existing implementers."),
        (M, "A class implements two interfaces that both declare void run(). What must the class do?", "Provide one implementation of run() that satisfies both", ["Nothing; the method is inherited automatically", "Implement run() twice with different bodies", "It cannot compile in Java"],
         "One method with that signature fulfils both contracts."),
        (H, "Can an interface extend other interfaces?", "Yes, it can extend more than one", ["Yes, but only one", "No, only classes can extend", "Only if they are abstract classes"],
         "Interfaces use extends and support multiple interface inheritance."),
    ],
    "Exception Handling": [
        (E, "Which block always executes whether or not an exception occurs?", "finally", ["catch", "try", "throws"],
         "The finally block runs to release resources even when an exception is thrown or caught."),
        (E, "Which keyword is used to explicitly throw an exception object?", "throw", ["throws", "raise", "catch"],
         "throw raises an exception; throws only declares that a method may raise one."),
        (M, "Which of the following is a checked exception?", "IOException", ["NullPointerException", "ArithmeticException", "ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException"],
         "IOException must be caught or declared; the others are unchecked RuntimeExceptions."),
        (M, "What is the output?\ntry {\n  int r = 10 / 0;\n  System.out.print(\"A \");\n} catch (ArithmeticException e) {\n  System.out.print(\"B \");\n} finally {\n  System.out.print(\"C \");\n}", "B C", ["A B C", "A C", "B"],
         "10 / 0 throws before A prints; the catch prints B and finally always prints C."),
        (H, "What is the output?\nstatic int f() {\n  try { return 1; }\n  finally { System.out.print(\"F \"); }\n}\nSystem.out.println(f());", "F 1", ["1 F", "1", "F"],
         "The finally block executes before f() actually returns, so F prints first, then main prints 1."),
    ],
}
